Load a protected system configuration file first

Ghostty's configuration comes from files the user can write, from the
command line, and on macOS from GHOSTTY_CONFIG_PATH. Any process running as
the user can change all of those, and a configuration can set the command
Ghostty runs. An administrator has no place for settings that the user's own
processes cannot override.

Load /etc/ghostty/config.ghostty (/private/etc on macOS) before anything
else, but only if nobody but root can change it. The path is resolved one
component at a time relative to checked, open directories: every entry must
be owned by root, every directory unwritable by group and others unless it
is sticky (such as /nix/store, which nix-darwin's /etc links point into),
the file unwritable by group and others, and on macOS no ACL may allow
changes. A missing file changes nothing, so Ghostty behaves as upstream.

Two settings are honored only in that file, captured right after it loads
so nothing later can change them:

  * config-default-files = false skips the user's configuration files, a
    file named by GHOSTTY_CONFIG_PATH, and theme lookup in the user's theme
    directory (themes are configuration files too).
  * config-cli-args = false (new) ignores configuration from command-line
    arguments, which any process can pass with `open --args`.

Once a system file is loaded, themes and shell integration come from the
resources directory found from the executable's location, never from
GHOSTTY_RESOURCES_DIR. Any process running as the user can set that
variable for the next launch; a theme file can set any key, and shell
integration scripts run in the user's shell.

A system file that fails the check, or cannot be read, is not loaded, and
Ghostty skips user files and command-line configuration too, since the file
may have meant to turn them off. It runs with the built-in defaults and
reports why in the configuration-errors window and the log. A build option,
-Dsystem-config=required, treats a missing file the same way.

The macOS app loads the system file itself before choosing between the
default files and GHOSTTY_CONFIG_PATH (new C API
ghostty_config_load_system_file), so this patch does not rely on
env-reset.patch.

